  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/exec_machdep.c:380-459  do_trap_user (called from trap.c)
      The illegal-instruction handler in do_trap_user (lines 434-444) blindly treats the FIRST illegal-instruction trap for any thread as a potential lazy-FPU enable, regardless of what caused the trap. It checks only pcb_fpflags & PCB_FP_STARTED and does not inspect stval/scause to distinguish FP instructions from vector/IME instructions. If the hardware has V/IME extensions but VS=OFF, a vector instruction would cause the handler to spuriously enable the FPU (setting FS=CLEAN) and retry, before correctly delivering SIGILL on the second fault. This works correctly today because VS is never enabled, but the handler's logic conflates 'any first illegal instruction' with 'possibly an FP instruction needing lazy enable', creating a fragile dependency on hardware-level trapping for non-FP illegal instructions.
